{"timestamp":"2026-09-01T08:00:24.575Z","ordinal":14150,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_088039b628d127e6016a96860c5cd487d18be4e7763b4d44cc","status":"completed","call_id":"call_n7iSZaRHcHXgp6vZKTkI2l1w","name":"exec","input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\\n@@\\n-- KV H2D-only and resident-decode-only are consistently faster in CD3. In the\\n-  serving-path steady-state H2D+decode measurement, N=8 is effectively tied\\n-  (0.065 versus 0.063 ms), while CD3 is 7.1%, 4.7%, and 16.9% faster at\\n-  N=64/256/1024 respectively.\\n+- KV H2D-only and resident-decode-only are consistently faster in CD3. In the\\n+  current one-stream standalone `decompress_batch` benchmark, however, P4Flat's\\n+  lower dispatch/preparation overhead makes its total 6--12% faster at\\n+  N=8/64/256; N=1024 is tied. A previously reported CD3 total win used\\n+  P4Flat's legacy 32,768-byte blob stride, not its current 25,600-byte default.\\n@@\\n-## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\\n-\\n-The input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Compression\\n-was performed on the CPU with one worker; no GPU encoder was called. The table\\n-uses the previously recorded steady-state serving-path split measurement, in\\n-which the loader's H2D/decode stream arrangement and paired K/V native dispatch\\n-are active. H2D-only and resident-decode-only were measured independently from\\n-the complete serving operation, so their medians should not be arithmetically\\n-added to reproduce the total.\\n-\\n-| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 serving total | P4 serving total | CD3 total change |\\n-|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n-| 8 | 0.016 | 0.030 | 0.028 | 0.046 | 0.065 | 0.063 | effectively tied (+0.002 ms) |\\n-| 64 | 0.026 | 0.042 | 0.029 | 0.047 | 0.065 | 0.070 | 7.1% faster |\\n-| 256 | 0.056 | 0.081 | 0.036 | 0.049 | 0.102 | 0.107 | 4.7% faster |\\n-| 1024 | 0.173 | 0.232 | 0.065 | 0.082 | 0.251 | 0.302 | 16.9% faster |\\n-\\n-All values are milliseconds. CD3 reduces isolated H2D by 46.7%, 38.1%,\\n-30.9%, and 25.4%, and isolated resident decode by 39.1%, 38.3%, 26.5%, and\\n-20.7% at the four sizes. The complete serving operation is effectively tied at\\n-N=8 and faster for CD3 at every larger measured size.\\n-\\n-A later three-round diagnostic initially placed here used independently\\n-constructed compressor objects with `load_kv_stream=None`. That exercises the\\n-standalone one-stream fallback rather than the loader-wired serving pipeline;\\n-its `decompress_batch` totals therefore must not supersede the serving-path\\n-comparison above. It remains useful only as an isolated dispatch diagnostic.\\n+## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\\n+\\n+The input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Each codec\\n+and block count ran in a new process with CPU encoding, one worker, 20 warm-ups\\n+and 100 CUDA-event samples. Three complete rounds were run; the table reports\\n+the median of the three per-process medians. No GPU encoder was called.\\n+\\n+This benchmark constructs a compressor directly and never constructs a\\n+`DualMemoryLoader`; consequently `load_kv_stream=None` and `decompress_batch`\\n+uses its standalone one-stream fallback. It is a codec/dispatch microbenchmark,\\n+not a full serving-pipeline measurement.\\n+\\n+| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 standalone total | P4 standalone total |\\n+|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n+| 8 | 0.0171 | 0.0289 | 0.0309 | 0.0458 | 0.0703 | 0.0629 |\\n+| 64 | 0.0260 | 0.0376 | 0.0287 | 0.0448 | 0.0686 | 0.0647 |\\n+| 256 | 0.0559 | 0.0666 | 0.0371 | 0.0466 | 0.1004 | 0.0915 |\\n+| 1024 | 0.1730 | 0.1849 | 0.0668 | 0.0736 | 0.2486 | 0.2497 |\\n+\\n+All values are milliseconds. CD3 reduces isolated H2D by 40.8%, 30.9%, 16.1%,\\n+and 6.4%, and isolated resident decode by 32.5%, 35.9%, 20.4%, and 9.2% at\\n+the four sizes. The complete one-stream call is 11.8%, 6.0%, and 9.7% slower\\n+at N=8/64/256 and tied at N=1024. P4Flat's C++ `decode_layer_prep` has lower\\n+fixed preparation/dispatch cost; at small N this outweighs CD3's faster copy\\n+and kernel in the complete-call timing.\\n+\\n+The earlier result remembered as N=8 tied and CD3 faster above N=8 was\\n+0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for CD3 versus\\n+0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms for P4Flat. It used P4Flat's legacy raw-safe\\n+`TIERED_SERVE_KV_BLOB_STRIDE=0`, which gives a 32,768-byte row. Current P4Flat\\n+defaults to a tight 25,600-byte row. Re-running legacy stride at N=1024\\n+reproduced H2D/decode/total = 0.229/0.081/0.300 ms, matching the old\\n+0.232/0.082/0.302 ms. Thus that ordering was a real result for the old P4Flat\\n+configuration, but it is not the correct comparison against current P4Flat.\\n*** Update File: /lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/CD3_CURRENT_MANIFEST.md\\n@@\\n - CPU-encoded production KV H2D+decode steady-state median on GH200 is\\n   0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for N=8/64/256/1024. P4Flat under the same\\n   split-measurement run is 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms. CD3 resident decode is\\n@@\\n   H2D is 0.016/0.026/0.056/0.173 ms versus 0.030/0.042/0.081/0.232 ms.\\n   The pre-blob CD3 values were\\n-  0.247/0.291/0.374/0.762 ms; all cases were bit-exact.\\n+  0.247/0.291/0.374/0.762 ms; all cases were bit-exact. This historical P4Flat\\n+  comparison used its raw-safe 32,768-byte blob stride. Current P4Flat defaults\\n+  to a 25,600-byte tight stride, so these P4Flat values must not be used as the\\n+  final current-codec comparison.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a05bfa-a835-7061-8e05-ada14243ce5d","create_time":1788249607.354293}}}
